T36.01|Payment systems transfer value using messaging, clearing and settlement arrangements. A fast customer notification does not by itself describe final legal settlement. Providers must manage authorisation, fraud, reconciliation and liquidity as well as user experience.~~Different rails have different finality, access and reversal rules. Study the actual system rather than treating every digital payment as the same process.|Initiation: Customer requests payment.;;Clearing: Instructions and obligations processed.;;Settlement: Value transferred under system rules.|A merchant sees ₹10,000 marked successful in an app but must reconcile its bank settlement.~~Match transaction identifiers and fees.;;Confirm credited amount and settlement status.;;Investigate discrepancies through authorised channels.~~Front-end status and accounting reconciliation are different checks.~~Actual finality depends on the rail and rules.|Map the full payment flow.;;Verify finality and reversals.;;Reconcile balances and fees.
T36.02|Card payments involve cardholders, merchants, issuing banks, acquiring providers and networks. Authorisation confirms an approved transaction under rules; clearing and settlement follow. Interchange, network and acquiring charges compensate different roles and may be regulated differently by country.~~Credit, fraud and chargeback risks are allocated across participants. A merchant discount rate should not be mistaken for the issuer's entire income.|Issuer: Provides the cardholder relationship.;;Acquirer: Supports merchant acceptance.;;Network/interchange: Scheme infrastructure and interparticipant pricing.|A merchant sale is ₹1,000 with an assumed 2% total merchant charge.~~Merchant receives ₹980 before other adjustments.;;The ₹20 is allocated under provider agreements.;;Do not assume all ₹20 is interchange.~~One customer-facing charge can contain several components.~~Rates and allocation here are illustrative.|Identify each participant.;;Separate fee components.;;Assess fraud, credit and chargeback exposure.
T36.03|UPI is an Indian payment system enabling participating account-to-account payments through defined interfaces and rules. Apps, banks and the underlying payment infrastructure perform different roles. The interface may be simple while authentication, routing, reconciliation and risk controls remain complex.~~Current limits, features and dispute procedures should be checked with NPCI and the relevant provider. Avoid assuming every app balance is a bank deposit.|Payer/payee bank: Holds the relevant accounts.;;App/interface: Initiates and presents payment functions.;;System infrastructure: Routes and processes under scheme rules.|A user pays ₹1,000 from a bank account through a participating app.~~Identify the debited and credited accounts.;;Check the transaction status and reference.;;Use official dispute channels for a failed or inconsistent entry.~~The app interface is distinct from the account holding funds.~~Specific features and rules can change.|Use official system information.;;Verify account and transaction records.;;Protect authentication credentials.
T36.04|Wallets store or represent value under a specific legal structure; remittance services transfer funds, including across borders. Customer balances may have different safeguarding and deposit protections from bank accounts. Cross-border transfers combine exchange pricing, fees and local payment rails.~~Compare the amount actually received and delivery time. A zero transfer-fee claim can conceal a wide currency spread.|Stored-value wallet: Balance under provider terms.;;Remittance: Transfer between locations or people.;;FX spread: Difference embedded in conversion pricing.|A customer sends USD1,000; mid-rate is hypothetical ₹80, provider rate ₹78.5 and explicit fee zero.~~Recipient gets ₹78,500.;;Mid-rate equivalent is ₹80,000.;;Implicit pricing difference is ₹1,500.~~Zero stated fee does not mean zero economic cost.~~Delivery and protection should be assessed separately.|Compare received amount.;;Check safeguarding and access.;;Review fees, spreads and delivery conditions.
T36.05|Payment gateways transmit and facilitate transaction information; aggregators can combine merchant onboarding and payment acceptance or settlement under applicable definitions. Providers' roles vary by country and business model. Reliability, reconciliation and handling of customer funds are central concerns.~~Transaction volume is not revenue. Pricing, processing costs, refunds and fraud losses determine the economics.|Gateway: Technology for payment routing and processing.;;Aggregator: Bundled acceptance and merchant services under local rules.;;Settlement service: Handles payment proceeds according to terms.|A provider processes ₹100 lakh at 1% revenue take rate and incurs ₹0.7 lakh variable processing cost.~~Revenue is ₹1 lakh.;;Contribution is ₹0.3 lakh before fixed costs and losses.;;Reconcile merchant funds separately from company revenue.~~Processed volume is not owned income.~~Principal-versus-agent and local definitions require review.|Identify fund-handling responsibilities.;;Separate volume and revenue.;;Measure reliability, losses and reconciliation.
T36.06|Digital lending uses technology for origination, assessment, servicing or distribution. Alternative data may improve coverage but can introduce bias, privacy problems and unstable correlations. Faster approval is not evidence of sound underwriting.~~Portfolio economics require defaults, recoveries, funding and acquisition costs. Model monitoring must track changing borrower populations and fraud patterns.|Origination technology: Customer acquisition and processing.;;Credit model: Estimates repayment and risk.;;Servicing/collections: Manages payments after origination.|A ₹100 lakh loan cohort earns ₹12 interest but loses ₹5 principal, pays ₹4 funding and ₹2 operating costs.~~Net contribution is ₹1.;;Test higher loss rates and collection timing.;;Review who owns the loans and risk.~~Gross yield greatly overstates net lending economics.~~Compliance and data-use permissions are jurisdiction-specific.|Measure cohort losses.;;Validate data and fairness.;;Map lender, platform and servicing obligations.
T36.07|Embedded finance integrates financial services into another product's customer journey. Banking-as-a-service arrangements provide infrastructure through licensed and technology partners, but responsibilities for deposits, lending and compliance must be explicit. Interface branding does not determine the regulated entity.~~Dependence on a partner can create operational and business-continuity risk. Customers need clear information about who holds funds and handles complaints.|Embedded payment: Payment within a nonfinancial journey.;;Embedded credit: Financing at a purchase or workflow point.;;BaaS partnership: Bank capabilities delivered through partners.|A retail app offers accounts through a partner bank and charges ₹1 lakh service revenue.~~Separate the app's revenue from customer deposit balances.;;Identify the entity holding funds.;;Review continuity if the partnership ends.~~Commercial integration does not erase legal roles.~~Protections depend on actual structure and local rules.|Map regulated responsibilities.;;Explain custody and protection clearly.;;Stress partner failure or termination.
T36.08|Open banking and account aggregation share financial data through authorised arrangements and consent frameworks. They can improve financial visibility and service access, but data permission is different from permission to move money. Scope and legal roles differ across systems.~~Consent should specify purpose, duration and accessible information. Secure interfaces and revocation procedures reduce risk without guaranteeing perfect security.|Data access: Reads specified financial information.;;Payment initiation: Requests movement under separate authority.;;Consent management: Controls purpose, scope and duration.|A user grants three months of read-only bank-data access for loan assessment.~~Confirm which accounts and fields are shared.;;Do not infer transfer authority from read consent.;;Review revocation and retention terms.~~Information sharing and payment control are distinct.~~Current permissions follow the relevant framework and provider.|Use explicit scoped consent.;;Verify provider authorisation.;;Review revocation, security and retention.
T36.09|Robo-advice automates elements of financial assessment, portfolio selection and maintenance. Algorithms can improve consistency and lower servicing costs, but output quality depends on inputs, assumptions and service scope. Automation does not guarantee suitability or performance.~~Questionnaires may miss important liabilities or unusual circumstances. Human escalation and understandable explanations improve decision support.|Automated allocation: Maps inputs to portfolio weights.;;Automated maintenance: Rebalances under rules.;;Hybrid advice: Combines software with human review.|A questionnaire captures age and risk preference but omits a ₹10 lakh payment due next year.~~Identify the missing liquidity constraint.;;Reassess the proposed allocation with the obligation.;;Use appropriate escalation rather than accepting output blindly.~~Incomplete inputs can produce a mathematically valid but unsuitable plan.~~Actual advice duties vary by provider and jurisdiction.|Check service scope.;;Supply complete constraints.;;Review fees, assumptions and escalation options.
T36.10|Insurtech, regtech and wealthtech apply technology to insurance, compliance and investment services respectively. Their value depends on improved outcomes, efficiency or access rather than the label itself. Domain rules and human accountability remain important.~~A tool may automate collection or checking while leaving the final decision with a regulated entity. Evaluate false positives, false negatives and operational integration.|Insurtech: Supports insurance distribution or operations.;;Regtech: Supports regulatory and control processes.;;Wealthtech: Supports investing and financial management.|A compliance tool reviews 10,000 transactions and flags 200, of which 20 are confirmed concerns.~~Flag precision is 10%.;;Measure missed concerns through an appropriate evaluation.;;Assess investigation capacity and costs.~~Alert volume alone does not establish control quality.~~Ground-truth data and legal reporting duties require care.|Measure outcomes rather than activity.;;Validate error rates.;;Assign final decision ownership.
T36.11|Digital identity and fraud controls establish who is acting and whether an action is legitimate. Identity documents, device signals, authentication and transaction monitoring provide different evidence. Fraudsters can exploit genuine accounts or social engineering despite strong technical authentication.~~Controls should minimise unnecessary friction while detecting meaningful risk. Protect sensitive data and review both false approvals and false rejections.|Identity proofing: Establishes claimed identity.;;Authentication: Checks access by a recognised user.;;Fraud monitoring: Detects suspicious actions and patterns.|A valid user is deceived into approving a ₹50,000 transfer.~~Authentication can be correct while the payment is fraudulent.;;Review behavioural and destination risk signals.;;Provide clear warnings and incident procedures.~~Identity checks cannot prevent every authorised scam.~~Controls and liability depend on the service and local rules.|Separate identity from transaction intent.;;Monitor evolving fraud patterns.;;Protect data and recovery procedures.
T36.12|A distributed ledger records shared state across participants under a consensus or governance mechanism. Blockchains organise records in linked blocks; permissions determine who can read, validate or transact. Immutability claims should be examined alongside governance and technical assumptions.~~A ledger can preserve a false input accurately. It does not by itself establish ownership of an off-chain asset or solve every reconciliation problem.|Permissionless network: Participation broadly open under protocol rules.;;Permissioned network: Access restricted by governance.;;Smart contract: Code executing specified state changes.|A token record says a warehouse owns 100 units of goods, but the warehouse actually holds 80.~~Check the physical inventory independently.;;Evaluate who supplied the data.;;Review legal claims tied to the token.~~Reliable ledger history does not guarantee truthful external facts.~~Code and legal rights require separate analysis.|Assess consensus and governance.;;Verify off-chain inputs.;;Review security and enforceable rights.
T36.13|Cryptoassets and tokens can represent payment-like units, governance rights, utility access or claims on assets under varied structures. The token's name does not determine legal classification, intrinsic value or investor protection. Custody, protocol and market risks can be substantial.~~Analyse the actual rights and supply mechanisms. A rising market price does not prove productive cash generation or a secure ownership claim.|Native cryptoasset: Unit associated with a network.;;Utility/governance token: Access or decision rights under terms.;;Asset-linked token: Purports to represent an external claim.|A token supply doubles while total supported asset value stays ₹100 lakh.~~Value backing per token halves if claims are equal.;;Inspect issuance rights and redemption.;;Do not infer backing from the quoted price alone.~~Supply and claim design affect economic participation.~~Legal status and valuation depend on actual facts.|Read rights and issuance rules.;;Assess custody and code risks.;;Use current primary regulatory information.
T36.14|Stablecoins seek a reference value through reserves or other mechanisms; central-bank digital currencies are digital central-bank liabilities under their design. Neither label establishes identical access, privacy or settlement features. A target peg is not a guarantee that redemption always works.~~Reserve quality, liquidity, legal redemption rights and operating controls determine resilience. Algorithmic mechanisms can behave differently from fully reserved claims.|Reserve-backed stablecoin: Relies on specified reserve assets.;;Algorithmic arrangement: Uses market or protocol incentives.;;CBDC: Digital liability of a central bank.|A stablecoin has 100 units issued and reserve assets marked at 100, but an urgent sale yields only 90.~~Immediate liquidation coverage is 90%.;;Assess cash redemption rights and available liquidity.;;Consider how a run changes asset sales.~~Book backing and executable redemption capacity differ.~~This is a hypothetical stress, not a claim about a specific issuer.|Verify reserves and redemption rights.;;Assess liquidity and custody.;;Distinguish private issuers from central-bank liabilities.
T36.15|DeFi uses protocols for activities such as exchange and collateralised lending. Automated market makers price trades through pool rules; lending protocols manage collateral and liquidation through code and data feeds. Decentralised branding does not remove governance, oracle, smart-contract or liquidation risk.~~Liquidity-provider returns combine fees with changing asset exposure. Impermanent loss compares pool value with simply holding the initial assets, and can become realised when exiting.|AMM: Formula-based pooled exchange.;;Collateralised lending: Borrowing secured under protocol rules.;;Oracle: Supplies external prices or information.|A simple constant-product pool starts with 10 units A and 1,000 units B, so product is 10,000.~~If A falls to 8 units after a trade, B becomes 1,250 ignoring fees.;;The trader adds 250 B and removes 2 A.;;Compare fees and price impact with the pool rule.~~Trades change both reserves and marginal pricing.~~Real pools may use different formulas and face code risks.|Read protocol mechanics.;;Stress collateral and oracle failure.;;Compare fees with exposure and contract risks.
T36.16|Tokenisation represents specified rights or records through digital tokens. It can improve transfer and automation, but underlying ownership, custody, redemption and legal enforceability remain essential. Tokenising an illiquid asset does not automatically create liquid demand.~~Separate technical transferability from regulatory permission and market depth. Dividing an asset into many units changes denomination, not necessarily risk or value.|Asset token: Represents a stated asset claim.;;Security token: Rights subject to relevant securities law.;;Record token: Digital representation without necessarily transferring ownership.|A ₹100 lakh property is represented by 10,000 tokens under an illustrative arrangement.~~Nominal asset allocation is ₹1,000 per token.;;Review whether tokens actually grant enforceable property or entity rights.;;Inspect debt, costs and transfer restrictions.~~Small denomination does not establish direct ownership or liquidity.~~Legal documentation determines the claim.|Verify rights and custody.;;Reconcile tokens with underlying assets.;;Assess market depth and transfer rules.
T36.17|Fintech business economics depend on the activity: payments earn service margins, lending bears credit and funding risk, and investment platforms may earn fees on assets or transactions. Customer funds should not be counted as company revenue. Growth requires controlled operations and regulatory capacity.~~Measure contribution after losses, incentives and partner costs. A high transaction count can coexist with negative unit economics.|Payments economics: Volume, take rate and processing cost.;;Lending economics: Yield, losses and funding cost.;;Platform economics: Fees, acquisition and retention.|A payment app processes ₹1 crore, earns 0.5%, pays processing ₹30,000 and incentives ₹40,000.~~Revenue is ₹50,000.;;Contribution is −₹20,000 before fixed costs.;;Test whether repeat use lowers incentives sustainably.~~Volume growth alone can increase losses.~~Regulatory and partner constraints also affect scaling.|Use activity-specific metrics.;;Separate customer balances from revenue.;;Stress losses, partners and regulatory costs.
